The function does a few field assignments and at most one stream seek per call, so it is unlikely to be a meaningful part of the 270 ms. The change below is a small cleanup with identical behavior. The seek and `clear()` order is kept, because swapping them would change behavior on a failed stream.

```cpp
void Decode::InitRead(std::istream &file, FIT_BOOL startOfFile)
{
    fileBytesLeft = 3; // Header byte + CRC.
    fileHdrOffset = 0;
    crc = 0;
    headerException = "";
    // Only reset to header state if we do not want
    // to skip the header.
    if (skipHeader == FIT_FALSE)
        state = STATE_FILE_HDR;
    lastTimeOffset = 0;

    // Reset to the beginning of the file
    if (startOfFile == FIT_TRUE)
    {
        file.seekg(0, std::ios::beg);
    }

    file.clear(); // workaround libc++ issue
}
```

```includes
#include <ios>
```